values ('b1c2d3e4-5f6a-4c41-8d01-2e3f4a5b6c11', 'policing-in-america', 'Policing in America', 'Policing in America situates law enforcement as an evolving public authority shaped by local government, race, labor, immigration, social control, reform movements, and public safety demands. Students study history and mission, organization and governance, recruitment and culture, patrol and calls for service, discretion, constitutional limits on stops, searches, arrests, and interrogation, investigations and intelligence, use of force, community and problem-oriented policing, race and legitimacy, accountability, crime data and evidence-based practice, technology and surveillance, specialized contexts and vulnerable populations, officer wellness and ethics, homeland security and federalism, and reform policy design.');

